  (705, 'uk', 'Absinthe fountain', 'A vital attribute in the ritual of serving the strong French drink. The water along with herbs and fruit and sometimes with ice, is poured into the main flask. Once brewed, the drink pours drop by drop through sugar on a spoon and ends up in the glass with absinthe.'),
  (698, 'uk', 'Absinthe spoon', 'A vital attribute in the ritual for serving the strong French drink. The alcohol with herbs and fruit is poured into the main flask. Once brewed, the drink pours drop by drop through sugar on the spoon and ends up in the glass.'),
  (494, 'uk', 'Energy drink', 'Energy Drink has been specifically designed for use during increased physical and mental activity.'),
  (394, 'uk', 'Irish cream liqueur', 'Irish cream liqueur made from Irish whiskey that is triple distilled, with addition of cream and neutral alcohol. Enriched with cocoa, vanilla, sugar and caramel.'),
  (547, 'uk', 'Blueberry', 'Little purple berry that grows on bushes in Canada, North America, Turkey and Western Europe. It contains a large amount of vitamins A, B, C, manganese and potassium. It is useful for the eyesight, improves night vision. One bar spoon contains 5 g of berries.'),
  (578, 'uk', 'Lemon grass', 'Grass with citrus aroma with origins in the Caribbean and Asian cuisine, grows mainly in the British Isles, the Philippines and Indonesia.'),
  (490, 'uk', 'Sprite', 'Sweet carbonated drink produced using soda with addition of sugar and lemon extract.'),
  (666, 'uk', 'Sambuca', 'Italian liquor produced by flavoring of wheat alcohol with sugar and oils of ordinary and star anise with extracts from berries, elderberry flowers and various herbs, that produce its lasting fragrance.'),
  (414, 'uk', 'Advocaat liqueur', 'Dutch Christmas liqueur descended from Brazilian alcoholic beverage made from avocado. This drink is made from a mixture of egg yolks, sugar, vanilla and brandy.'),
  (531, 'uk', 'Pomegranate', 'Mediterranean fruit growing on a fruit-bearing deciduous shrub or small tree grown primarily in Israel and India. Contains potassium and phosphorus, know to have a positive effect on the cardiovascular system. One fruit weighs on average 300 g and can be cut into 10 slices.'),
  (684, 'uk', 'Red wine glass', 'Classic, slightly curved glass. Traditionally higher than white wine glass. Standard capacity: 200-300 ml.'),
  (483, 'uk', 'Cherry juice', 'For preparing the juice, cherries are put under a press and then filtered and bottled.'),
  (551, 'uk', 'Blackcurrant', 'Little black berry with its bushes growing in Central, Northern Europe, and North Asia. It contains large amounts of vitamin C, various sugars, organic acids and minerals. One bar spoon contains 5 g of berries.'),
  (597, 'uk', 'Raspberry jam', 'Jam of red berries growing on perennial dwarf shrubs mainly in Russia, Serbia, Poland and the United States. Ripe fruits are boiled with sugar until a homogeneous state. A bar spoon can hold 5 g jam.'),
  (590, 'uk', 'Orange jam', 'Jam from the pulp of citrus fruits growing on tall trees mainly in Brazil, USA and China. Ripe fruits are boiled with sugar until a homogeneous state. A bar spoon can hold 5 g jam.'),
  (589, 'uk', 'Honey', 'Flower nectar collected and processed by bees.'),
  (652, 'uk', 'Cookie', 'A sweet biscuit, baked in an oven from different types of dough.'),
  (686, 'uk', 'Pitcher', 'An ancient kitchen receptacle for serving water, lemonade and sangria. Standard volume: 500-3000 ml.'),
  (484, 'uk', 'Pomegranate juice', 'For preparing the juice, pomegranate seeds are put under a press and then filtered and bottled.'),
  (548, 'uk', 'Watermelon', 'A large type of berries, planted mainly in China, Turkey and Iran. Contains glucose, fructose, pectin, calcium, magnesium, alkaline substance, normalizes metabolism. One berry weighs an average of 5,000 g, a slice - 300 g, the ratio of the weight of the pulp to peel - 60% to 40%.'),
  (454, 'uk', 'Lemongrass syrup', 'Lemon syrup produced by pasteurizing lemongrass extracts with sugar syrup.'),
  (770, 'uk', 'Milkshake sundae glass', 'Previously used to serve ice cream today is used to serve milkshakes, milkshake sundae, soft drinks and cocktails. Standard capacity: 8-11 oz.'),
  (433, 'uk', 'Dry white wine', 'Summer wine produced by maceration and subsequent fermentation of skinless white grapes. Then the wine is filtered, kept in oak barrels for at least 3 months.'),
  (532, 'uk', 'Kiwi', 'The kiwifruit or Chinese gooseberry is the edible berry of a woody vine growing in Italy, New Zealand and Chile. Contains vitamin C, fibre, folic acid, calcium and phosphorus, known to enhance human immune system. One berry weighs on average 120 g.'),
  (577, 'uk', 'Red basil', 'Spicy aromatic herbs, growing mainly in the warm regions of the Old World.'),
  (702, 'uk', 'Cream whipper', 'A device made out of thick glass or metal for quickly whipping cream into a delicate and airy consistency. It can also be used for making various desserts, complex sauces and mousses. Standard volume: 0.5-1 litre'),
  (399, 'uk', 'Peach liqueur', 'Sunny fruit liqueur produced by distilling fermented peach juice with the addition of cognac, to enhance the rich flavour peach pits may be added.'),
  (396, 'uk', 'Apricot liqueur', 'Famous fruit liqueur produced by distillation of the fermented apricot juice with strong spirits to enhance the rich flavour.'),
  (691, 'uk', 'Punch bowl', 'A large receptacle made out or glass, crystal or metal for serving punch which was brought to England from India in the beginning of the 17th century. Standard volume: 5 litre.'),
  (395, 'uk', 'Amaretto liqueur', 'A classic Italian liqueur produced by maceration of apricot pits, different herbs and fruit in neutral alcohol. The resulting base is filtered, mixed with sugar and diluted with water to the desired strength.'),
  (556, 'uk', 'Cherry', 'Yellow or scarlet berry that grows on trees mainly in warm and temperate climates, such as Turkey, Italy, Spain, Chile. Contains organic acids, minerals, pectin, folic acid, vitamins A, C, E, B. One berry weighs an average of 5 g.'),
  (437, 'uk', 'Lager beer', 'Light beer made from water, barley, malt extract, hops and brewer''s yeast.'),
  (452, 'uk', 'Blackcurrant syrup', 'Berry syrup produced by processing the juice of ripe black currant berries mixed with sugar and water.'),
  (397, 'uk', 'Melon liqueur', 'Tropical Asian liqueur produced by maceration of melon pulp in neutral spirit with water. The result is filtered, adjusted to the desired sweetness and strength and bottled.'),
  (763, 'uk', 'Bitter lemon', 'Carbonated lemon drink produced by carbonating water with lemon extract.'),
  (380, 'uk', 'Becherovka', 'Canonical Czech liquor produced by maceration of a blend of 20 herbs and spices in neutral spirit, which is subsequently mixed with water and sugar and is aged for two to three weeks in oak casks.'),
  (502, 'uk', 'Earl Grey tea', 'In its classic form, this is a black tea with the addition of oil made from the rind of the bergamot fruit. The best temperature for brewing this tea is 90-95°С and it should be left to stand for 3-5 minutes.'),
  (709, 'uk', 'Cocktail umbrella', 'Bright paper decoration for tropical cocktails or to entertain children.'),
  (639, 'uk', 'Prune', 'A European delicacy produced from black plums dried in the sun or in a special dryer.'),
  (747, 'uk', 'Tartlet', 'A miniature copy of a tart, that was first mentioned in King Richard II cooking book in 1390. Baked dish consisting of a filling over a pastry base with an open top not covered with pastry.'),
  (453, 'uk', 'Yellow apple syrup', 'Yellow, fruity syrup produced by pasteurizing juice of yellow apples with the addition of cane sugar.'),
  (576, 'uk', 'Rosemary', 'Mediterranean herbs with a spicy scent of pine.'),
  (409, 'uk', 'Grapefruit liqueur', 'Citrus liqueur produced by maceration of peel of pink grapefruit in neutral alcohol. The resulting spirit is filtered, mixed with sugar and diluted with pure water to a required strength.'),
  (549, 'uk', 'Cranberry', 'A small red berry that grows on dwarf shrubs or trailing vines mainly in the United States, Chile, Argentina and the Netherlands. Contains organic acids, minerals, pectin, folic acid, and vitamins A, C, E, B. One bar spoon contains 5 g of berries.'),
  (492, 'uk', 'Tonic', 'Carbonated drink that was invented in India to combat malaria. It is made from carbonated water mixed with quinine and a small amount of sugar.'),
  (562, 'uk', 'Red grapes', 'Red berries of medium size; a grape is a fruiting berry of the deciduous woody vines. Comes from China, the US, Italy, France and Spain. Contains sugar, cellulose, organic acids, vitamin B and other trace elements, has a strengthening and toning effect. One berry weighs an average of 5 g.'),
  (663, 'uk', 'Orange vodka', 'A strong flavored spirit produced by multiple distillation of grains, which are then subject to several stages of carbon and other filtering. The result is mixed with clean water, and infused with extracts of ripe orange.'),
  (530, 'uk', 'Tangerine', 'Tangerine is an orange-coloured citrus fruit that grows on evergreen shrubs mainly in China, Spain and Brazil. Contains vitamin C and potassium, known to enhance immunity. One fruit weighs around 100 g and yields 40 ml juice.'),
  (710, 'uk', 'Lillet Blanc', 'French aperitif wine produced from a combination of the white wines of the Bordeaux region mixed with fruit liqueurs. While the wine is maturing, peel of sweet, bitter and sour oranges and green quinine is infused in brandy spirits. Then all is mixed and kept in oak barrels for 8-12 months.'),
  (389, 'uk', 'Spiced rum', 'Spirits of a Caribbean produced via vertical distillation of the alcohol obtained by fermentation of molasses or juice from sugar cane. Before blending kept in burnt oak barrels of bourbon or sherry from 2 to 7 years, depending on the chosen method of aging. Infused with cinnamon, nutmeg, vanilla and other spices.'),
  (398, 'uk', 'Blue curacao liqueur', 'Neon orange liqueur produced by maceration of dried orange peel in a neutral alcohol with water. The result is filtered, mixed with sugar and coloured with natural dyes.'),
  (550, 'uk', 'Green grapes', 'Green berries of medium size; a grape is a fruiting berry of the deciduous woody vines. Comes from China, the US, Italy, France and Spain. Contains sugar, cellulose, organic acids, vitamin B and other trace elements, has a strengthening and toning effect. One berry weighs an average of 5 g.'),
  (533, 'uk', 'Mango', 'Wild tropical fruit growing primarily in India, Pakistan and the Philippines. Contains vitamin A, folic acid and potassium, assists in improving immunity and sight. One fruit weighs on average 300 g and can be cut into 10 slices.'),
  (408, 'uk', 'Cinnamon liqueur', 'German spiced liqueur produced by maceration of cinnamon in schnapps. The result is filtered, mixed with sugar and gold leaf is added for the zing.'),
  (495, 'uk', 'Green iced tea', 'Popular in Asia, iced tea is produced on the basis of the green tea extract and other herbs without any artificial flavors and preservatives.'),
  (711, 'uk', 'Julep strainer', 'An indispensable accessory when working with a mixing glass. Also serves as an unmatched decoration for the classic cocktail of the same name. Used for filtering cocktails of crushed pieces of ice and other undesired ingredients.'),
  (670, 'uk', 'Espresso coffee', 'A strong coffee which is prepared by pouring a small quantity of boiling water at a temperature of 90 °C through ground coffee.'),
  (690, 'uk', 'Tea cup', 'Traditional Turkish tea drinkers cup. Standard volume: 40-80 ml.'),
  (412, 'uk', 'Passion fruit liqueur', 'Made from the pulp of ripe passion fruit, oranges and grapefruit, which are mixed with alcohol and allowed to infuse.'),
  (615, 'uk', 'Vanilla granulated sugar', 'Classic sugar flavored with vanilla.'),
  (181, 'uk', 'French press', 'French accessory for coffee and tea brewing, consists of a glass flask with a strainer and a press.'),
  (448, 'uk', 'Strawberry syrup', 'Berry syrup produced by pasteurizing juice from ripe strawberries mixed with sugar and water.'),
  (567, 'uk', 'Chili pepper', 'Hot tropical peppers growing on bushes mainly in South America. Contains Vitamins A and C as well as potassium, magnesium, and iron. A pod weighs an average 30 g'),
  (499, 'uk', 'Black tea', 'A type of tea which has undergone fermentation for a period of between two weeks and a month. Grows in regions with a tropical or subtropical climate. For brewing, pour 100 ml of boiling water on 1 spoonful of tea and leave for 4-5 minutes.'),
  (411, 'uk', 'Mango liqueur', 'Asian tropical liqueur produced by maceration of the pulp ripe mangoes in neutral alcohol with water. The resulting alcohol is filtered and mixed with sugar.'),
  (514, 'uk', 'Vanilla ice cream', 'The frozen dessert is prepared from milk, cream, butter and sugar with the addition of vanilla extract. Stored in the freezer for no more than a month.'),
  (592, 'uk', 'Cherry jam', 'Jam of yellow or scarlet berries growing mainly in warm temperate climates, such as Turkey, Italy, Spain and Chile. Ripe pitted cherries are boiled with sugar until homogenized. A bar spoon holds 5 g jam.'),
  (485, 'uk', 'Peach juice', 'For preparing the juice, peaches are put under a press and then filtered and bottled.'),
  (387, 'uk', 'Blackcurrant vodka', 'Strong flavored spirit produced by multiple distillation of grains, subject to several stages of carbon and other filtering, result is mixed with clean water. The resultant drink infused with extracts of ripe black currant.'),
  (633, 'uk', 'Rosewater', 'Antique recipe of scented water infused with rose petals.'),
  (713, 'uk', 'Raspberry puree', 'Raspberry puree'),
  (586, 'uk', 'Thyme', 'Aromatic spice consisting of dwarf shrubs, growing mainly in Eurasia, North Africa and Greenland. Used in Provençal mixes, ideal for flavoring strong spirits.'),
  (407, 'uk', 'Coconut liqueur', 'Tropical Tiki liqueur produced by maceration of coconut pulp in rum spirits. The resulting spirit is filtered, mixed with sugar and diluted with water to the desired strength.'),
  (430, 'uk', 'Rose vermouth', 'The relatively young category of vermouth produced by maceration of alpine herbs and spices in a pink wine. The result is filtered, adjusted to the desired strength and sweetness and aged in oak barrels for at least a year.'),
  (646, 'uk', 'Dark chocolate', 'Confectionery dessert made of dark cocoa beans with little to no sugar added.'),
  (447, 'uk', 'Caramel syrup', 'The famous caramel syrup produced by processing liquid caramel with addition of vanilla and water.'),
  (750, 'uk', 'Napkins', 'Cotton or paper serviettes. Sometimes used as coasters for drinks.'),
  (360, 'uk', 'Soda syphon', 'Device made out of thick glass or metal for carbonating drinks. Standard volume: 1-2 litre.'),
  (500, 'uk', 'Whole bean coffee', 'Roasted beans of coffee tree, due to their caffeine content known to have a stimulating effect on the human body. There are two main varieties of coffee tree - Arabica and Robusta.'),
  (565, 'uk', 'Sake', 'Japanese liquor made from rice that is soaked and steamed. The prepared culture is enriched with fermentation fungi and kept in a warm, humid room for about 2 days. After fermentation sake kept in sealed containers from 6 to 12 months.'),
  (537, 'uk', 'Kumquat', 'Small citrus fruit growing of small fruit-bearing trees mainly in China, Korea and Japan. Contains vitamin C, iron and magnesium, assists in the fighting infections. One fruit weighs an average 10g.'),
  (685, 'uk', 'Cognac glass', 'Classic glass for drinking cognac or brandy. Standard capacity: 170-400 ml.'),
  (423, 'uk', 'Southern Comfort', 'New Orleans fruit liqueur invented by bartender Martin Heron in 1874, produced by an ancient recipe based on American whiskey with the addition of fruit and herbal essences, sugar and caramel and aged in oak barrels until the desired maturity.'),
  (703, 'uk', 'Frying pan', 'An ancient cooking utensil necessary in the bar for flambéing berries and fruit.'),
  (688, 'uk', 'Tasting set', 'A tasting set. It consists of a glass tray filled with ice, ensuring the tasting shots are kept cool.'),
  (462, 'uk', 'Lychee syrup', 'Asian fruit syrup produced by pasteurizing lychee juice mixed with sugar syrup and water.'),
  (504, 'uk', 'Chamomile tea', 'A brew made from the dried flowers of the chamomile plant. Generally, boiling water is poured over the flowers in a proportion of 1-2 teaspoons to 200 ml of water, it is then left to stand for 7-10 minutes, strained and then either drank as it is or with the addition of sugar or honey.'),
  (673, 'uk', 'Sea buckthorn jam', 'Jam of small yellow berries growing in deciduous thorny shrubs mostly on the Atlantic coast of Europe and in North-West China. Ripe fruits are boiled with sugar until a homogeneous state. A bar spoon can hold 5 g jam.'),
  (689, 'uk', 'Sake set', 'A set for the ritual serving of the strong Japanese alcohol. Average volume of the tokkuri flask: 250-300 ml, average volume of the cup: 50-60 ml.'),
  (769, 'uk', 'Saucer', 'Glass, porcelain or metal type of small dishware that is served classically with a cup. In modern interpretation a cocktail glass with a saucer projects a more refined and elegant service.'),
  (463, 'uk', 'Mango syrup', 'Asian fruit syrup produced by pasteurizing mango juice mixed with cane sugar and water.'),
  (700, 'uk', 'Tea glass holder', 'A metal glass holder with a handle which holds the glass allowing one to drink a hot drink without burning one’s hands.'),
  (695, 'uk', 'Fork', 'Cutlery consisting of a handle and several prongs (usually between two and four) on one end. Used for preparing food as well as while eating for holding or moving parts of the meal.'),
  (950, 'uk', 'Thermometer', 'A device for measuring the temperature of liquids, the environment, the body, etc.'),
  (981, 'uk', 'Vacuum coffee maker', 'Coffee brewer that uses vapor pressure to make the drink.'),
  (445, 'us', 'Cinnamon syrup', 'The famous spiced syrup, produced by pasteurizing and infusing sugar syrup with cinnamon.'),
  (176, 'us', 'Lime juice', 'To prepare the juice, limes are put under a press, then the juice is filtered and bottled.'),
  (482, 'us', 'Apple juice', 'To prepare the juice, apples are put through a press, then filtered and bottled.'),
  (767, 'us', 'Quail egg white', 'To make this cocktail ingredient with a gentle and foamy texture, we recommend "dry shaking." One chicken egg white equals 5 quail egg whites.'),
  (217, 'us', 'Cucumber', 'Cucumbers are creeping vines that bear cylindrical fruits and grow mainly in the Mediterranean countries. They regulate the metabolism and improve heart and kidney function. One cucumber weighs an average of 5 oz, and can be cut into 10 slices or strips.'),
  (31, 'us', 'Ice cubes', '"To produce this indispensable ingredient, water purity and the speed of freezing must be closely monitored. The slower the water is frozen, the clearer the final product will be. An ideal specimen has no stripes and melts slowly in the glass. \r\n"'),
  (210, 'us', 'Champagne saucer', '''A glass with a tall stem and wide bowl. According to legend, it was designed to resemble the silhouette of one of the King of France''s mistresses. Used to serve champagne and tini cocktails. Standard volume: 7-10 oz.'''),
  (68, 'us', 'Shaker', '"It took its current form at the end of the 1800s, and earned especial popularity in the 1920s and 1930s through dozens of different styles and forms. They are divided into categories: cobbler shakers, which have a built-in strainer; French shakers, without a strainer; and Boston shakers, which consist of two equal-sized cups. Standard volume: 15—25 oz."'),
  (69, 'us', 'Strainer', 'An accessory indispensable when working with Boston or French shakers. Used for filtering fruit pits, pieces of ice and other undesired ingredients out of drinks.'),
  (73, 'us', 'Jigger', '''Ensures accurate and ideal portions in cocktails. The best bartenders always have a range of sizes in their arsenal: 0.5-1oz, 0.25-0.75 oz and 1-2 oz.'''),
  (70, 'us', 'Squeezer', 'An indispensable tool for squeezing juice from limes and lemons. Despite the fact that most popular establishments nowadays use pre-prepared juice, freshly-squeezed juice is a mark of higher quality.'),
  (239, 'us', 'Juicer', 'Extracts juice from any vegetable or fruit. Unlike with a hand press, an electric mechanism separates the juice from the pulp using a spinner.'),
  (1, 'us', 'Vodka', 'Vodka'),
  (55, 'us', 'Coffee liqueur', 'A famous invigorating liqueur produced by percolating coffee beans of different varieties with grain alcohol. The resulting mix is not filtered, but mixed directly with burnt sugar and diluted with water to the desired strength.'),
  (65, 'us', 'Rocks glass', '''A Wide glass with a thick bottom, used to serve strong spirits or cocktails in a pure, undiluted form. Standard capacity: 6-10 oz.'''),
  (64, 'us', 'Bar spoon', 'This indispensable bartender''s tool is used for preparing cocktails in a mixing glass and for making layers in shots, as well as to measure 0.15 oz portions of liquids or dry ingredients.'),
  (57, 'us', 'Drinking straws', 'In 1888, Marvin Stone, the owner of a paper mouthpiece factory, invented the best accessory for drinking drinks from glasses. Joseph Friedman then made a fortune by developing flexible drinking straws. The final step was to make straws from plastic.'),
  (799, 'us', 'London dry gin', 'Spirit of English origin. Produced in vertical pot stills, alcohol is infused for days with juniper and ten other herbs and spices, and then distilled for about 7 hours and bottled.'),
  (54, 'us', 'Triple sec', 'A cult classic "triple dry" citrus liqueur, produced by macerating curacao orange peel in grain alcohol with water. The result is triple distilled before bottling in order to maintain high strength.'),
  (9, 'us', 'Lemon juice', 'To prepare the juice, lemons are put under a press, then the resulting mass is filtered and bottled'),
  (734, 'us', 'Lemon zest', 'The top layer of lemon skin, cut with a special sharp knife. It contains essential oils rich in lemon flavor. Zest can be obtained from a cut or whole fruit, then grated or even twisted into a spiral (called the twist).'),
  (66, 'us', 'Sour glass', '"Sour glasses were designed to hold Sour cocktails, which contain a base liquor, lemon or lime juice, and a sweetener. Standard capacity: 3-4 oz."'),
  (71, 'us', 'Zest knife', '"A bartending tool sourced from the Japanese culture of carving. Used to cut off thin layers of zest, or make rings or twists from apples."'),
  (143, 'us', 'Mezcal', 'A Mexican liquor, made using traditional methods like smoking agave in underground pits on hot volcanic rocks. This infuses the drink with smoky flavor. Then the agave hearts are milled, and the juice is fermented and aged in oak barrels for at least 4 months.'),
  (224, 'us', 'Green basil', 'A fragrant and piquant herb that grows mainly in warm climates of the Old World. Its green leaves have gained widespread popularity thanks to the "Basil Smash," which has already become a classic.'),
  (223, 'us', 'Cardamom', 'A Ceylon seasoning that grows on herbaceous plants of the ginger family. It is considered one of the most intense spices in the world.'),
  (566, 'us', 'Ginger root', 'An Asian perennial, imported to Europe in the Middle Ages. Its rhizome looks like rounded pieces, and is useful for improving immunity and fighting headaches. One slice of the root weighs an average of 0.15 oz.'),
  (83, 'us', 'Crushed ice', '''The first “shaved” ice was cut from large sheets by bartenders using special knives. Later, crushed ice makers and ice machines appeared. '''),
  (117, 'us', 'Sieve', 'Used together with shakers and strainers for double filtration of cocktails when pouring them from the shaker into the glass. With a finer net than a strainer, it does not allow even the smallest fruit seeds and bits of ice to fall into the drink.'),
  (52, 'us', 'Muddler', '"An indispensable accessory for tropical cocktails, this crushes berries and fruits either in the shaker or directly in the glass."'),
  (373, 'us', 'Clothespins', 'A decorative accessory used to hold garnishes to the glass.'),
  (113, 'us', 'Absinthe', 'A French spirit produced by macerating herbs and spices, including lemon balm, fennel and anise in grain or wine alcohol. The main component is wormwood, which contains Thujone, a highly intoxicating ingredient.'),
  (295, 'us', 'Simple syrup', 'Simple syrup'),
  (152, 'us', 'Raspberry', 'A small red berry that grows on perennial subshrubs mostly in Russia, Serbia, Poland and the United States. It contains sugar, organic acids, vitamins A, B, C, and fatty oils. One berry weighs an average of 0.15 oz.'),
  (125, 'us', 'Highland single malt whisky', 'A spirit of Scottish origin produced in the mountainous Highlands region of Scotland. Made from 100% barley malt by double or, rarely, triple distillation. The resulting alcohol is matured for at least 3 years in oak barrels.'),
  (47, 'us', 'Red bitter', 'The world-renowned Italian bitters produced using a secret recipe starting 1860. A set of aromatic herbs and citrus fruit, it contains 40 to 70 ingredients, including bitter orange, sweet flag, gentiana and perhaps rhubarb.'),
  (189, 'us', 'Grapefruit juice', 'To prepare the juice, grapefruits are put under a press, then filtered and bottled.'),
  (672, 'us', 'Mint', 'The most widely recognized herb in cocktails, it has more than 30 species. Peppermint and sweet mint are primarily used in mixology. Store for no longer than two days in the refrigerator, wrapped in a paper towel.'),
  (371, 'us', 'Swizzle stick', 'A small stick made of wood gathered from the twigs of the branches of the chupa-chupa, whose heads form a sort of mixer. They are ideal for mixing cocktails served with crushed ice directly in the glass.'),
  (124, 'us', 'Green chartreuse', 'This monastic liqueur has been produced in the French Alps using a secret recipe since 1764. About 130 plants and herbs are macerated in neutral alcohol and distilled several times. The result is balanced with sugar, then mixed to the desired strength and aged in oak barrels.'),
  (427, 'us', 'Elderflower liqueur', 'A French floral liqueur produced by macerating crushed elderberry flowers using a secret recipe. The resulting spirit is filtered, mixed with sugar and diluted with water to the desired strength.'),
  (35, 'us', 'Dry vermouth', 'A light Italian aperitif, first presented in 1900, but for which an ideal recipe demanded 10 years of hard work. MARTINI EXTRA DRY is based on a complex bouquet of various exotic plants, thanks to which it has a unique taste and a delicate aroma containing notes of raspberry and lemon on a background of iris, with light touches of wood and sherry. All of this is topped off by a balanced, fresh aftertaste with a touch of tartness. This drink''s unique flavor makes it an irreplaceable component of many cocktails.'),
  (130, 'us', 'Dry orange liqueur', 'Dry Orange Curaçao is a dark orange liqueur, flavored with distillates of Curaçao orange peels and a hint of rum. The remaining mass is filtered, mixed with sugar, and aged in oak barrels.'),
  (114, 'us', 'Peychaud''s bitters', 'A classic New Orleans bitters, invented by the famous apothecary in 1830, is made according to a secret recipe by infusing neutral spirits with anise, cloves, vanilla, nutmeg, fruit and spices.'),
  (161, 'us', 'Goblet glass', '''A royal glass with thick walls, permitting the clinking of glasses while drinking without the risk of breaking. Standard capacity: 6-10 oz.'''),
  (665, 'us', 'Maraschino liqueur', 'A berry liqueur from Croatia, made by macerating maraschino cherries with pits in neutral spirits with water for at least a month. The resulting mix is then filtered, sweetened, brought to the required strength and aged in oak barrels for at least three years.'),
  (303, 'us', 'Grapefruit bitters', 'A seasonal summer bitters produced by infusing neutral spirits with grapefruit peel. Hops are added to soften the bitterness.'),
  (20, 'us', 'Red maraschino cherry', 'Special varietals of cherry, such as "King Anna", "Rainer" and "Golden Diversity" are placed in a saline solution, then soaked in sugar syrup with added food colouring.'),
  (96, 'us', 'Cocktail skewer', 'A decorative accessory for serving olives in martinis and cocktail cherries in Manhattans. First appeared in bars at the end of the 19th century, thanks to the marketing genius Charles Forster.'),
  (101, 'us', 'Aged rum', 'A spirit of Caribbean origin, produced via vertical distillation of the alcohol resulting from molasses or sugar cane juice fermentation. Before blending, it is kept in burnt oak barrels from either bourbon or sherry for 7 to 20 years, depending on the chosen method of aging.'),
  (116, 'us', 'Dark cacao liqueur', 'A dark chocolate liqueur produced by the percolation of cocoa beans with a neutral alcohol. The resulting spirit is diluted with water to the desired strength and mixed with Madagascan vanilla and caramel, which gives the liqueur a dark colour.'),
  (67, 'us', 'Mixing glass', '''A classic must-have for a five-star bar. Used to prepare strong, masculine drinks without added juices, herbs or jams. Ice keeps its form and does not melt, maintaining the drink''s strength. Standard capacity: 16-30 oz.'''),
  (166, 'us', 'Amaro', 'An Italian digestive liqueur produced by macerating herbs and roots, including citrus peel, juniper, fennel, lemon balm, anise and cardamom in neutral alcohol. The resulting spirit is filtered, mixed with sugar and diluted with water to the desired strength.'),
  (171, 'us', 'Ice ball', '"The pride of Japanese bartenders: ice elevated to a work of art. Today they are more common in bars thanks to special machines. Prepared ice cubes are placed into a warming vice. Then the metal is heated to cut out the contours of a sphere."'),
  (3, 'us', 'Lemon', 'A tropical citrus fruit that grows on small evergreen trees mainly in China, India and Mexico. Contains vitamin C and organic acids, and is traditionally used to fight colds and nutritional deficit. One fruit weighs 6 oz and yields 2 oz of juice, and can be cut into 8 slices or 4 large segments of 0.5 oz each.'),
  (62, 'us', 'Bourbon', 'Strong spirit of American origin, according to the law, made of at least 51% corn malt with addition of barley and rye, distilled and filtered. Aged for your pleasure in oak barrels from 2 to 7 years. The barrels are burnt from the inside using a special technology.'),
  (111, 'us', 'Creme de cassis liqueur', 'A berry liqueur from Dijon, produced by macerating black currants in grain alcohol with water for up to two months. The liqueur is not filtered and mixed with sugar.'),
  (63, 'us', 'Angostura bitters', 'The recipe created by Dr. Johann Gottlieb Benjamin Siegert remains a secret to this day. During the preparation process, spices are infused in 97% alcohol, then diluted with cane sugar and distilled water.'),
  (16, 'us', 'Light cream', 'Light cream is made from cow''s milk by separating the cream from milk. The percentage of fat varies in different countries, but is usually not more than 15%. Can be stored in the refrigerator for up to 20 days.'),
  (36, 'us', 'Red vermouth', 'This famous vermouth began thanks to the combination of wine, alcohol, sugar, and 35 Alpine herbs and spices. Created in 1863 by Luigi Rossi himself, the original Martini is an icon of Italy in and of itself, and a symbol of Italian aperitivo culture. The dry, spicy notes of MARTINI Rosso are complemented brilliantly by tonic and an orange slice - such a drink pairs perfectly with a light dinner with friends.'),
  (34, 'us', 'Club soda', 'Carbonated water, which became an indispensable component of fizzes and other carbonated drinks, was previously produced by carbonating water with carbon dioxide. Now, it is more and more often prepared by adding baking soda to purified still water.'),
  (732, 'us', 'Orange zest', 'The top layer of orange skin, cut with a special sharp knife. It contains essential oils rich in orange flavor. Zest can be obtained from a cut or whole fruit, then grated or even twisted into a spiral (called the twist).'),
  (258, 'us', 'Prosecco', 'According to the 2009 Appellation, the Prosecco name can be applied onto to vineyards of this specific region. Glera grapes are gathered by hand and subjected to fermentation in a vacuum, which allows the flavor to be preserved for much longer. Sparkling wine is distinguished by its expressive aroma, with notes of white spring flowers, green apple and citrus, and has a pleasant taste with a gentle fruit overtone and subtle mineral aftertaste. Ideal when paired with seafood and hard cheeses.'),
  (148, 'us', 'Peach puree', 'Peach puree'),
  (535, 'us', 'Peach', 'A sun-loving fruit that grows on deciduous trees mainly in China, Italy and Spain. Contains organic acids, mineral salts and pectin. One fruit weighs an average of 4 oz, which yields around 2.5 oz of mash.'),
  (59, 'us', 'Flute', '''A high-society glass for cocktails featuring champagne or sparkling wine. The long, narrow form is reminiscent of a tulip and holds the bubbles in the cocktail, allowing it to remain fizzy for longer. Standard volume: 6-8 oz.'''),
  (179, 'us', 'Lime cordial', 'An ancient citrus recipe, produced by pasteurizing lime juice mixed with sugar syrup, cream of tartar and water.'),
  (733, 'us', 'Lime zest', 'The top layer of lime skin, cut with a special sharp knife. It contains essential oils rich in lime flavor. Zest can be obtained from a cut or whole fruit, then grated or even twisted into a spiral (called the twist).');

